{"name":"Wood Thrush","hex":"#a47d43","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"wood thrush","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A species of bird, Hylocichla mustelina."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wood_thrush"}],"entries":[{"title":"Wood thrush","blocks":[{"text":"The wood thrush is a North American passerine bird in the family Turdidae and is the only species placed in the genus Hylocichla. It is closely related to other thrushes such as the American robin and is widely distributed across North America, wintering in Central America and southern Mexico. The wood thrush is the official bird of the District of Columbia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Wood_thrush_%28Hylocichla_mustelina%29_Peten.jpg/330px-Wood_thrush_%28Hylocichla_mustelina%29_Peten.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wood_thrush"},{"title":"Thrush (bird)","blocks":[{"text":"The thrushes are a passerine bird family, Turdidae, with a worldwide distribution. The family was once much larger before biologists reclassified the former subfamily Saxicolinae, which includes the chats and European robins, as Old World flycatchers. Thrushes are small to medium-sized ground living birds that feed on insects, other invertebrates, and fruit. Some unrelated species around the world have been named after thrushes due to their similarity to birds in this family."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Psophocichla_litsitsirupa_%28Etosha%29.jpg/330px-Psophocichla_litsitsirupa_%28Etosha%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thrush_(bird)"},{"title":"Wood","blocks":[{"text":"Wood is a natural, lignocellulosic material derived primarily from the secondary xylem of trees and other woody plants, where it provides mechanical support and in the functional sapwood, conducts water and dissolved minerals. Being a natural material, it is characterized as an organic material – a natural composite of cellulosic fibers that are strong in tension and embedded in a matrix of lignin and hemicelluloses that resists compression."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/16_wood_samples.jpg/330px-16_wood_samples.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wood"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Wood_thrush_gathering_nesting_material.jpg/500px-Wood_thrush_gathering_nesting_material.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Wood_thrush_gathering_nesting_material.jpg/1280px-Wood_thrush_gathering_nesting_material.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Wood_thrush_gathering_nesting_material.jpg/500px-Wood_thrush_gathering_nesting_material.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Wood thrush with nesting material in Michigan, USA","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWood_thrush_gathering_nesting_material.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Hylocichla_mustelina_-John_Heinz_National_Wildlife_Refuge_at_Tinicum%2C_Pennsylvania%2C_USA_-family-8.jpg/330px-Hylocichla_mustelina_-John_Heinz_National_Wildlife_Refuge_at_Tinicum%2C_Pennsylvania%2C_USA_-family-8.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Hylocichla_mustelina_-John_Heinz_National_Wildlife_Refuge_at_Tinicum%2C_Pennsylvania%2C_USA_-family-8.jpg/330px-Hylocichla_mustelina_-John_Heinz_National_Wildlife_Refuge_at_Tinicum%2C_Pennsylvania%2C_USA_-family-8.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&u